Noah (Sales): FYI Kai's team saw SSE streaming break during their POC demo. EventSource fires `error` and the app shows `Unexpected end of input` when parsing. Happens frequently behind their corporate firewall.

Eva (Solutions Eng): Do we have a raw fragment from the browser network tab or console? Also which SDK are they using?

Kai (Client Eng): Pasted from Chrome (redacted):
```
data: {\"req\":\"req-42\",\"part\":\"{\\\"out\\\":\\\"A multi-byte char: \"\n
```
Next event arrives as:
```
data: {\"req\":\"req-42\",\"part\":\"\\u00e9\\\"}\n
```
They call `JSON.parse(event.data)` per message which fails when a multibyte char splits across messages. SDK: `@redwood/browser-sdk@2.0.0` from CDN.

Eva (Solutions Eng): Classic proxy/edge problem: gzip or buffer/coalesce splitting chunks, or Cloud proxy re-chunking mid-character. Quick triage checklist: (1) confirm `content-type: text/event-stream` (2) see if `content-encoding: gzip` is present (3) run `curl -N -v` to capture raw stream and boundaries.

Kai (Client Eng): curl shows `content-encoding: gzip` injected by their corporate proxy. When I reproduce locally with gzip on the proxy, the client sees fragmented JSON frequently. Turn gzip off and stream stabilizes.

Noah (Sales): They're worried about changing firewall/proxy settings for the demo. Any fast fallbacks?

Eva (Solutions Eng): Fast options for a demo: 1) Force websocket transport in the SDK for the demo: `new Redwood({ transport: 'websocket' })` — uses wss over 443 so usually allowed. 2) Client-side reassembly: buffer event.data fragments per request id until they form valid JSON (simple brace-balancing or id marker). 3) Upgrade to the latest SDK patch if we shipped incremental UTF-8 buffering.

Kai (Client Eng): They can't change proxy. Can you share a tiny reassembly snippet I can drop into their app?

Eva (Solutions Eng): Sure — small heuristics work for demos:
```
const buffers = {};
es.addEventListener('message', e => {
  // each event.data is like: {\"req\":\"req-42\",\"part\":\"...\"}
  const p = JSON.parse(e.data); // safe if each event is full meta
  buffers[p.req] = (buffers[p.req] || '') + p.part;
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buffers[p.req]);
    handle(obj); delete buffers[p.req];
  } catch (err) {
    // still partial, keep buffering
  }
});
```
Note: this assumes `part` is payload chunk. If they can't safely JSON.parse meta, they can extract id via regex.

Noah (Sales): Any security questionnaire notes about websockets/TLS that I can forward to their infra team? They asked about ports and audit logs.

Eva (Solutions Eng): For Hosted: websockets are wss over 443, no firewall changes usually needed. Sessions are authenticated via tokens and we log connection/traces in the same audit pipeline as SSE. For Dedicated: might need to open outbound/inbound based on their network, but it's standard. I'll DM you a 2-paragraph blurb.

infra-bot: Deployed patch stream-utf8-fix v2026.10.12 by @eva 3:18Z  includes improved incremental UTF-8 buffering in browser-sdk v2.0.3.

Kai (Client Eng): I'll try the reassembly snippet and websocket fallback for the demo. Will also send HAR + `curl -N -v` output so you can confirm chunk boundaries.

Noah (Sales): Great — please ping me once tested. If websocket works we'll use that for the live walkthrough. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
